 {:db/id "q-115261" :question/id #uuid "1aea0f50-2894-540a-8561-3e930ff1c7d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-115261" :question/problem "Given that the tangent line approximation to the function $f(x)$ at $x=\\sqrt{2}$ overestimates the values of the function in the neighborhood of $x=\\sqrt{2},$ which of the following must be true?" :question/worked-solution "Since the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=\\sqrt{2}$ overestimates the values of the function, we have\n\n$$\nL(x)>f(x),\n$$\n\nthat is, the line $L(x)$ must be above the graph of $f(x)$ near the point  $x=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the function must be concave down in the neighborhood of $x=\\sqrt{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-115261/ma-source-field/b18e64ee2988097fc3900b4afb8cf7ee6e17ca7eb2818933fd3d82d931269081"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
