 {:db/id "q-123316/ma-source-field/970149316a550392eee9863b122fc32990f9a6c679336541f4c12ae6f15345e8" :answer-field/id #uuid "b405d2fb-dd7e-50bf-8a2a-780bcd6b3b54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-123316/ma-source-field/970149316a550392eee9863b122fc32990f9a6c679336541f4c12ae6f15345e8/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-123316/ma-source-field/970149316a550392eee9863b122fc32990f9a6c679336541f4c12ae6f15345e8/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-123316/ma-source-field/970149316a550392eee9863b122fc32990f9a6c679336541f4c12ae6f15345e8/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" "q-123316/ma-source-field/970149316a550392eee9863b122fc32990f9a6c679336541f4c12ae6f15345e8/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-123316/ma-source-field/970149316a550392eee9863b122fc32990f9a6c679336541f4c12ae6f15345e8/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25"] :answer-field/correct "q-123316/ma-source-field/970149316a550392eee9863b122fc32990f9a6c679336541f4c12ae6f15345e8/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-123316" :question/id #uuid "fd4fed36-e750-5401-88f4-2ac9e7e598e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-123316" :question/problem "Which of the following sequences diverge?\n\nI. ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{3}}{1-{n}^{2}}$ for $n\\ge 1$\nII. ${a}_{n}=\\frac{3}{n}$ for $n\\ge 1$\nIII. ${a}_{n}=\\frac{2{n}^{2}-3{n}^{3}}{{n}^{3}+5}$ for $n\\ge 1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each sequence in turn. We need to calculate the limit of each sequence as $n$ approaches infinity.\n\n- In sequence I,  the dominant term in the denominator is $-{n}^{2},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{n}^{3}}{1-{n}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{{n}^{3}}{{n}^{2}})}{(\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}-\\frac{{n}^{2}}{{n}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{n}{(\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}-1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{n}{0-1} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit does not exist, so the sequence diverges.\n\n- In sequence II, the limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{3}{n}=0.\n$$\n\nThe limit exists, so the sequence converges.\n\n- In sequence III, the dominant term in the denominator is ${n}^{3},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & =\\frac{2{n}^{2}-3{n}^{3}}{{n}^{3}+5} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2{n}^{2}}{{n}^{3}}-\\frac{3{n}^{3}}{{n}^{3}})}{(\\frac{{n}^{3}}{{n}^{3}}+\\frac{5}{{n}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2}{n}-3)}{(1+\\frac{5}{{n}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-3}{1+0} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit exists, so the sequence converges.\n\nIn conclusion, sequence I diverges." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-123316/ma-source-field/970149316a550392eee9863b122fc32990f9a6c679336541f4c12ae6f15345e8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
