 {:db/id "q-123458/ma-source-field/3c66f47172289ae24e626ed40b66f693ac14294e53e87920e4073b375ac3dd85" :answer-field/id #uuid "5aaf3974-45a7-5d36-b332-ebb301287f47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-123458/ma-source-field/3c66f47172289ae24e626ed40b66f693ac14294e53e87920e4073b375ac3dd85/65494cbacf729c0fd4818bf37b98bdf4170be69ee1b00cbbdd79d388a1c06851" "q-123458/ma-source-field/3c66f47172289ae24e626ed40b66f693ac14294e53e87920e4073b375ac3dd85/1df3623a82ad993faa050736f6f1aec50a2af4a5427533c1078ad12647a0e5b2" "q-123458/ma-source-field/3c66f47172289ae24e626ed40b66f693ac14294e53e87920e4073b375ac3dd85/6036074d3f4f325411e05eb8bd961ca2b57e4dd5fbcb3cca4c221f37cd84df8a" "q-123458/ma-source-field/3c66f47172289ae24e626ed40b66f693ac14294e53e87920e4073b375ac3dd85/de09ec3b9e53fa585686d5cca81fd750edff8ee72e4376b75b1583e72755287a" "q-123458/ma-source-field/3c66f47172289ae24e626ed40b66f693ac14294e53e87920e4073b375ac3dd85/7ce0233c98a168bff4d1dcbfd8dd5fe56a3487e705dd4becb87199257294181b"] :answer-field/correct "q-123458/ma-source-field/3c66f47172289ae24e626ed40b66f693ac14294e53e87920e4073b375ac3dd85/7ce0233c98a168bff4d1dcbfd8dd5fe56a3487e705dd4becb87199257294181b" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-123458" :question/id #uuid "9debd71f-7b2d-5a32-ae10-ca946736e9d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-123458" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }\\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi $ ?" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }\\,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta }{\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta })^{2}+{(\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta }{\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta )+\\frac{4{cos}^{2}⁡4\\theta }{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{\\frac{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta )^{2}+4(1-{sin}^{2}⁡4\\theta )}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{\\frac{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta )^{2}+4(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta )(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta )}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{\\frac{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta )[1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta +4-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta ]}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{5-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-123458/ma-source-field/3c66f47172289ae24e626ed40b66f693ac14294e53e87920e4073b375ac3dd85"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
