 {:db/id "q-126539/ma-source-field/2b403d7b52643675dde2ee615551ae924e323ce0aa0a741d65b24aa2ff554586" :answer-field/id #uuid "18f53d24-ffcb-51fb-8ef8-920f533f7ad1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-126539/ma-source-field/2b403d7b52643675dde2ee615551ae924e323ce0aa0a741d65b24aa2ff554586/4e73bc2cbb0398aed7a6404daffd64caf03fea8cae3288212de9c43383f5da9a" "q-126539/ma-source-field/2b403d7b52643675dde2ee615551ae924e323ce0aa0a741d65b24aa2ff554586/d1daf42bd090aa46df741d57d51284a29cba8c3f31e1e4a0de4a41a899eac7a2" "q-126539/ma-source-field/2b403d7b52643675dde2ee615551ae924e323ce0aa0a741d65b24aa2ff554586/0fc180b3da721ea5eddbc52ca5795c55a003dcff9670118045d19b82723f450b" "q-126539/ma-source-field/2b403d7b52643675dde2ee615551ae924e323ce0aa0a741d65b24aa2ff554586/82d200f397478943aa8e99437fcc75839dd211e2cddf6fdc504ab01bcd6826a6" "q-126539/ma-source-field/2b403d7b52643675dde2ee615551ae924e323ce0aa0a741d65b24aa2ff554586/f1a467fb62cee3da3343e38ab339acdd673436617a35fbedec1906bea3e1335b"] :answer-field/correct "q-126539/ma-source-field/2b403d7b52643675dde2ee615551ae924e323ce0aa0a741d65b24aa2ff554586/f1a467fb62cee3da3343e38ab339acdd673436617a35fbedec1906bea3e1335b" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-126539" :question/id #uuid "a9b65d2f-1d10-5cc6-91ba-5ed12d5b8509" :question/math-academy-id "q-126539" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,dx+2{x}^{2}y\\,dy={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=36$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/c2/c2718471780b5ea4f4b0298624d14a22efb317ef4e8302b9269eeeec9449ca0d.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=x{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=2{x}^{2}y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=4xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,dx+2{x}^{2}y\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4xy-2xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 6,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}2\\cdot r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}2{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{6}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-126539/ma-source-field/2b403d7b52643675dde2ee615551ae924e323ce0aa0a741d65b24aa2ff554586"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
