 {:db/id "q-126564/ma-source-field/eff08f97adf523be1119cc5b6ac6215d4965a8ff70c5d9fc2df4e303fdf88e6b" :answer-field/id #uuid "9ca86920-5215-52e0-b0cb-aabc47b3bb45" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-126564/ma-source-field/eff08f97adf523be1119cc5b6ac6215d4965a8ff70c5d9fc2df4e303fdf88e6b/cba457d1177c014d48b1be93b2a6806f207c5caa217c73610fe2390d6400a97c" "q-126564/ma-source-field/eff08f97adf523be1119cc5b6ac6215d4965a8ff70c5d9fc2df4e303fdf88e6b/2dfc27e64edad57fa001a5f9e2ad266c059f9248f5ec54d7e82205cb663e57b6" "q-126564/ma-source-field/eff08f97adf523be1119cc5b6ac6215d4965a8ff70c5d9fc2df4e303fdf88e6b/511b4df7a9f1c1c1016f724d2b10d1a2cb104f82212d884b4ccb20abf46db369" "q-126564/ma-source-field/eff08f97adf523be1119cc5b6ac6215d4965a8ff70c5d9fc2df4e303fdf88e6b/f984089476c67eba1cf09aaf977d1d04ff8d5a7d396b1eac7c6cb90dc077687c" "q-126564/ma-source-field/eff08f97adf523be1119cc5b6ac6215d4965a8ff70c5d9fc2df4e303fdf88e6b/4bedc533d7f031e976995dae41f1b37fc3d8b3cd50af960606450b0d67c16ed9"] :answer-field/correct "q-126564/ma-source-field/eff08f97adf523be1119cc5b6ac6215d4965a8ff70c5d9fc2df4e303fdf88e6b/f984089476c67eba1cf09aaf977d1d04ff8d5a7d396b1eac7c6cb90dc077687c" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-126564" :question/id #uuid "88dc642a-08f7-5754-86d1-6d7942af6e8c" :question/math-academy-id "q-126564" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $F(x,y)=⟨xy-\\sqrt[3]{x+5},\\,x+2y⟩$ and $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(xy-\\sqrt[3]{x+5})\\,dx+(x+2y)\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/79/798665574b8f0e11d991bb0d934efd01bdeb65840c2e86498be4edbc59578439.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=xy-\\sqrt[3]{x+5}$ and $Q(x,y)=x+2y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(xy-\\sqrt[3]{x+5})\\,dx+(x+2y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-x)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(1-x)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(1-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}r-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-126564/ma-source-field/eff08f97adf523be1119cc5b6ac6215d4965a8ff70c5d9fc2df4e303fdf88e6b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
