 {:db/id "q-161862/ma-source-field/e58ad8bb49c692ae01e46460338a52d4b6fb754c550d073cacf1db83083a82c3" :answer-field/id #uuid "c5aa772d-380e-5267-8549-8e7d64f48e80" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-161862/ma-source-field/e58ad8bb49c692ae01e46460338a52d4b6fb754c550d073cacf1db83083a82c3/e4a36a4fea245e2ace6ca07b6e322e1b8c6c57ed5d3ba13e224bee8bb3dea982" "q-161862/ma-source-field/e58ad8bb49c692ae01e46460338a52d4b6fb754c550d073cacf1db83083a82c3/b047f3f836d3e1f06217a311edb483233c0e9c41372454ae6a41999e205cd9e0" "q-161862/ma-source-field/e58ad8bb49c692ae01e46460338a52d4b6fb754c550d073cacf1db83083a82c3/e50a1e5157a05f5237fc731110ab2dbf38a20076d05fd90c1acc758f696513ab" "q-161862/ma-source-field/e58ad8bb49c692ae01e46460338a52d4b6fb754c550d073cacf1db83083a82c3/8ebfceb5935c183358a1c71334deaa7dfeb4b8bb15ed855836887d5f174bb37d" "q-161862/ma-source-field/e58ad8bb49c692ae01e46460338a52d4b6fb754c550d073cacf1db83083a82c3/d419b77b8234755b2de64b889aada26f97b467f5011a9b237029a0d429f932c2"] :answer-field/correct "q-161862/ma-source-field/e58ad8bb49c692ae01e46460338a52d4b6fb754c550d073cacf1db83083a82c3/e50a1e5157a05f5237fc731110ab2dbf38a20076d05fd90c1acc758f696513ab" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id [:question/id #uuid "74d7a79e-d57c-5768-84fc-b3ae4dae67d9"] :question/problem "![](images/e6/e61f3d7c23ef7876bec2f11c93974551058bcd32f28745898604926d84dbcfee.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨y,\\,0,\\,2x⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of the solid onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=0$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper plane ${S}_{1}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is upward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨y,0,2x⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower cone ${S}_{2}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2)=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2)=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=y,\\,Q=0,\\,R=2x.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-y\\cdot \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-0\\cdot \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+2x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-2x)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2x\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}(\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-2x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-161862/ma-source-field/e58ad8bb49c692ae01e46460338a52d4b6fb754c550d073cacf1db83083a82c3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
