 {:db/id "q-161863/ma-source-field/c16583185a3b8e5f5d1e0024db8cf53cf5453a6dc7be6e2e828c0f076c8dec67" :answer-field/id #uuid "b8698323-72a0-5edf-895c-233cb6848a85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-161863/ma-source-field/c16583185a3b8e5f5d1e0024db8cf53cf5453a6dc7be6e2e828c0f076c8dec67/19087b21958de7cb1435ffacf0841b27eeb652fa6e05f18f0709e463c2064f30" "q-161863/ma-source-field/c16583185a3b8e5f5d1e0024db8cf53cf5453a6dc7be6e2e828c0f076c8dec67/68f43dc54e45e77e6dc64f2b9f27c8301b925fb8b06e5e071087991550255243" "q-161863/ma-source-field/c16583185a3b8e5f5d1e0024db8cf53cf5453a6dc7be6e2e828c0f076c8dec67/9576a6d99c7d7c9c44e3b0cb83293e5a14ab09d6cf81c92031514986a45b9040" "q-161863/ma-source-field/c16583185a3b8e5f5d1e0024db8cf53cf5453a6dc7be6e2e828c0f076c8dec67/ac429c12275fb04fd1f2d54bb45cebfadac38fef65bb28747de45e8368786c17" "q-161863/ma-source-field/c16583185a3b8e5f5d1e0024db8cf53cf5453a6dc7be6e2e828c0f076c8dec67/ada7aa8473e732ec704d06d4b941be5e991b9424c5d9bec4a6de108852228d29"] :answer-field/correct "q-161863/ma-source-field/c16583185a3b8e5f5d1e0024db8cf53cf5453a6dc7be6e2e828c0f076c8dec67/19087b21958de7cb1435ffacf0841b27eeb652fa6e05f18f0709e463c2064f30" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id [:question/id #uuid "87ce70cc-a7a3-5bf0-ac23-45c7dbb8a56d"] :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=⟨1,\\,z,\\,0⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the parabolic cylinders $z=4{x}^{2}$ and $z=16-4{y}^{2}.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=16-4{y}^{2}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=4{x}^{2}$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper cylinder ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(16-4{y}^{2})=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(16-4{y}^{2})=-8y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=1,\\,Q=z=16-4{y}^{2},\\,R=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1\\cdot 0-(16-4{y}^{2})\\cdot (-8y)+0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(128y-32{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower cylinder ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4{x}^{2})=8x \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{x}^{2})=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=1,\\,Q=z=4{x}^{2},\\,R=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-1\\cdot 8x-4{x}^{2}\\cdot 0+0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}-8x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}8x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(128y-32{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}8x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}128y-32{y}^{3}+8x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-161863/ma-source-field/c16583185a3b8e5f5d1e0024db8cf53cf5453a6dc7be6e2e828c0f076c8dec67"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
