 {:db/id "q-161896/ma-source-field/120ea6d1c4634b86817f5783bb5d5c0801fa3fb26d4ab25e80fe499085dacad9" :answer-field/id #uuid "1886a1f4-707b-5268-9cc1-ed7effda4966" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-161896/ma-source-field/120ea6d1c4634b86817f5783bb5d5c0801fa3fb26d4ab25e80fe499085dacad9/1caf53916944beab9c295fdee7c8967817601ce4739fb98255a811f5c2840b16" "q-161896/ma-source-field/120ea6d1c4634b86817f5783bb5d5c0801fa3fb26d4ab25e80fe499085dacad9/229b04a9dfabc00a7de8084d256b0d0d5aaf9c28e890cdb8d072b1911803c844" "q-161896/ma-source-field/120ea6d1c4634b86817f5783bb5d5c0801fa3fb26d4ab25e80fe499085dacad9/7efaf8108c3e7aa3262e55b472391b651ca7d7b5502234192022eac780e3c1e4" "q-161896/ma-source-field/120ea6d1c4634b86817f5783bb5d5c0801fa3fb26d4ab25e80fe499085dacad9/608f6d39cbb3cd6f923fb244f2a0bae0d6cf58c78d38488a893571d09a0f9a75" "q-161896/ma-source-field/120ea6d1c4634b86817f5783bb5d5c0801fa3fb26d4ab25e80fe499085dacad9/af0ca14c74a8ec153905fe2c41bed4e80bf4b3cd0d640d20f64a4a315b7c7d53"] :answer-field/correct "q-161896/ma-source-field/120ea6d1c4634b86817f5783bb5d5c0801fa3fb26d4ab25e80fe499085dacad9/229b04a9dfabc00a7de8084d256b0d0d5aaf9c28e890cdb8d072b1911803c844" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-161896" :question/id #uuid "0d7ee053-a1f8-527b-9332-a3761ff9b930" :question/math-academy-id "q-161896" :question/problem "![](images/14/1422e6534c001214335dcddb8888f7e2e9344e440379c47da38514e9a3ef0bd8.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨z,\\,0,\\,2⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So, \n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=1-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=0$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper cone ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=z=1-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\\,Q=0,\\,R=2.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-(1-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})-0\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})+2\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-x+2\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower plane ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is downward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,-1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨z,0,2⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-2\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-x+2\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}-2\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-161896/ma-source-field/120ea6d1c4634b86817f5783bb5d5c0801fa3fb26d4ab25e80fe499085dacad9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
