 {:db/id "q-161897" :question/id #uuid "1fddd3a8-32de-55ef-b058-e60e47b22c87" :question/math-academy-id "q-161897" :question/problem "![](images/6a/6af3d84594e2b2648dcbbae05a64f03ef5a38c76f5a1ed835fc4c6b6f50ef97a.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨0,\\,-x,\\,-z⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-3\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So, \n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=3-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=0$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper cone ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=0,\\,Q=-x,\\,R=-z=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-3.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-0\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})-(-x)\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-3\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-3\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower plane ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is downward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,-1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨0,-x,-0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}0\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-3\\,\\text{d}A+0 \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-3\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-161897/ma-source-field/ab72692f44528440110d90b738be003382d54aa0157ed95d0ffa4190d8e0a153"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
