 {:db/id "q-161901/ma-source-field/ae19ea99fed95da37bfc2bedf1899706bcf33c5c1c9a4bbdb851a9f2112c35d2" :answer-field/id #uuid "55ff700f-c93e-5ab0-80b1-0ec031f0502f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-161901/ma-source-field/ae19ea99fed95da37bfc2bedf1899706bcf33c5c1c9a4bbdb851a9f2112c35d2/bdb55ad0f6473f62ba59d76a4871960e4310fa80b386ac52e6bc46351dd153cd" "q-161901/ma-source-field/ae19ea99fed95da37bfc2bedf1899706bcf33c5c1c9a4bbdb851a9f2112c35d2/a31764af807fc7967786a140d52ac60dc7f8411601bfc2aa570f83859db71038" "q-161901/ma-source-field/ae19ea99fed95da37bfc2bedf1899706bcf33c5c1c9a4bbdb851a9f2112c35d2/23d62e8b0bb92fbc70b73e51dac8de5a36c180d9b08d3bd954b7284577e62e79" "q-161901/ma-source-field/ae19ea99fed95da37bfc2bedf1899706bcf33c5c1c9a4bbdb851a9f2112c35d2/a7e87dc5e5ec99234a6e5b6b852a1a63668d1f1b067e3bf8755a93c3cee83b5e" "q-161901/ma-source-field/ae19ea99fed95da37bfc2bedf1899706bcf33c5c1c9a4bbdb851a9f2112c35d2/4d70a98975aede469ff3006f070b741be1ae47e7c1405fb98e38e4ba378b5492"] :answer-field/correct "q-161901/ma-source-field/ae19ea99fed95da37bfc2bedf1899706bcf33c5c1c9a4bbdb851a9f2112c35d2/23d62e8b0bb92fbc70b73e51dac8de5a36c180d9b08d3bd954b7284577e62e79" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-161901" :question/id #uuid "e3eea45a-e598-5192-b757-2e1b9a1153d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-161901" :question/problem "![](images/c7/c7f140ad2c5687db48171819e1efaa858701af881ebbee57ff835da569f30947.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨{z}^{2},\\,0,\\,y⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So, \n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=0$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper hemisphere ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP={z}^{2}=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2},\\,Q=0,\\,R=y.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}})-0\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}})+y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}+y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower plane ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is downward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,-1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨{z}^{2},0,y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}+y\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}-y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-161901/ma-source-field/ae19ea99fed95da37bfc2bedf1899706bcf33c5c1c9a4bbdb851a9f2112c35d2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
