 {:db/id "q-161903/ma-source-field/7bf61d979c16e37b6e3077642ef13f24ef93587f545f3aa0d510792430b30749" :answer-field/id #uuid "d8800b1d-0dbb-5dd9-8914-c007ddb67ebc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-161903/ma-source-field/7bf61d979c16e37b6e3077642ef13f24ef93587f545f3aa0d510792430b30749/0d04ae6d410bda02675303eeaf21d24093884ae59769c4fd91d89de2050e1007" "q-161903/ma-source-field/7bf61d979c16e37b6e3077642ef13f24ef93587f545f3aa0d510792430b30749/1de97639243118fe2a274f2b36c7fce214d295b8fe0b9d6a4051f01ddde87644" "q-161903/ma-source-field/7bf61d979c16e37b6e3077642ef13f24ef93587f545f3aa0d510792430b30749/db33d5e33d866816d978388239c446f055c045ab4ba2f3dfe758f5e924eb78a9" "q-161903/ma-source-field/7bf61d979c16e37b6e3077642ef13f24ef93587f545f3aa0d510792430b30749/f10bd8ca4d389454da07ba5e17801dd8ef8e6cd1578ef5319a9a0feb3f536bfa" "q-161903/ma-source-field/7bf61d979c16e37b6e3077642ef13f24ef93587f545f3aa0d510792430b30749/79d05933a3fa591e9bae61d916771b806b6b69bfe48ac4fa336831eca3fb8af7"] :answer-field/correct "q-161903/ma-source-field/7bf61d979c16e37b6e3077642ef13f24ef93587f545f3aa0d510792430b30749/79d05933a3fa591e9bae61d916771b806b6b69bfe48ac4fa336831eca3fb8af7" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-161903" :question/id #uuid "c8fd3ad8-a86b-542f-ab4a-6226468a4f6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-161903" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=⟨0,\\,-z,\\,2y⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the cone $z=4-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ and the plane $z=0.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=4-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=0$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper cone ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=0,\\,Q=-z=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-4,\\,R=2y.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(0)\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})-(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-4)\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})+2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(y-\\frac{4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3y-\\frac{4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider lower plane ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is downward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,-1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨0,-z,2y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-2y)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3y-\\frac{4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}(-2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y-\\frac{4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-161903/ma-source-field/7bf61d979c16e37b6e3077642ef13f24ef93587f545f3aa0d510792430b30749"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
