 {:db/id "q-161904/ma-source-field/bae1424f9044bb32a62b3db15834f0445ce04c7b370c0f79d8baf929fc6e3194" :answer-field/id #uuid "4cf9dad5-46da-52ad-9f58-b7b22be13881" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-161904/ma-source-field/bae1424f9044bb32a62b3db15834f0445ce04c7b370c0f79d8baf929fc6e3194/557bb2d912ba1f8092f63aa026dfee60c9ec4a1268d4063410a8aa069545385c" "q-161904/ma-source-field/bae1424f9044bb32a62b3db15834f0445ce04c7b370c0f79d8baf929fc6e3194/4ef11d2b7b8d4b157c0659f12e43c0a543e1a8cc2daea15ddb741210163b6c8f" "q-161904/ma-source-field/bae1424f9044bb32a62b3db15834f0445ce04c7b370c0f79d8baf929fc6e3194/ce09173e4b383d8653fbf54e49db07fea7c8181dcd788913f3cf591a66df4c2b" "q-161904/ma-source-field/bae1424f9044bb32a62b3db15834f0445ce04c7b370c0f79d8baf929fc6e3194/964eea9446770c849581128c5843dcfee6f465a8454fa968f440cf8ae42a6a53" "q-161904/ma-source-field/bae1424f9044bb32a62b3db15834f0445ce04c7b370c0f79d8baf929fc6e3194/999690e439e40b6cdf26f3d70b3362dcfe183a1d593133837a84a1a4c9defeee"] :answer-field/correct "q-161904/ma-source-field/bae1424f9044bb32a62b3db15834f0445ce04c7b370c0f79d8baf929fc6e3194/4ef11d2b7b8d4b157c0659f12e43c0a543e1a8cc2daea15ddb741210163b6c8f" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-161904" :question/id #uuid "150a753b-c416-5943-8cfa-2d89a5b17df4" :question/math-academy-id "q-161904" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=⟨-y,\\,0,\\,x⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the hemisphere $z=1-\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ and the plane $z=1.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=1$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=1-\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper plane ${S}_{1}.$\n\nSince the positive orientation is upward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨-y,0,x⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider lower hemisphere ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=-y,\\,Q=0,\\,R=x.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-(-y)\\cdot \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-(0)\\cdot \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}+x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}+x)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A-{∬}_{D}(\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}+x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x-\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-161904/ma-source-field/bae1424f9044bb32a62b3db15834f0445ce04c7b370c0f79d8baf929fc6e3194"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
