 {:db/id "q-161908/ma-source-field/26786b9518b2d04b1b51f18aca484f13e91fbc7127a40446f8f469d15c08e7a5" :answer-field/id #uuid "9854d104-9a4b-53d3-8285-a6f5f2377a4d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-161908/ma-source-field/26786b9518b2d04b1b51f18aca484f13e91fbc7127a40446f8f469d15c08e7a5/82826f8ecb5cbd70a7c95d58d98c89eaec6861a1e10b4d42d34ed3441476a115" "q-161908/ma-source-field/26786b9518b2d04b1b51f18aca484f13e91fbc7127a40446f8f469d15c08e7a5/e843eb0d4019f921f2a694c70fa3f32d2998d22af7d5c44a599c49901744afd1" "q-161908/ma-source-field/26786b9518b2d04b1b51f18aca484f13e91fbc7127a40446f8f469d15c08e7a5/c8342a70c8e62051c47712f0d2fb90e2cca2ae41fa32ebd616f49ff0ee9032f1" "q-161908/ma-source-field/26786b9518b2d04b1b51f18aca484f13e91fbc7127a40446f8f469d15c08e7a5/b70caf718087bb0020d5d5a4f9bc319fc74a954b52b99a75849c3ff5f7476285" "q-161908/ma-source-field/26786b9518b2d04b1b51f18aca484f13e91fbc7127a40446f8f469d15c08e7a5/fbff51082f68235b416393ac6b32c66c5e132ddfcfd5743992626770a2a66de8"] :answer-field/correct "q-161908/ma-source-field/26786b9518b2d04b1b51f18aca484f13e91fbc7127a40446f8f469d15c08e7a5/e843eb0d4019f921f2a694c70fa3f32d2998d22af7d5c44a599c49901744afd1" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-161908" :question/id #uuid "883193fd-f5be-5d1c-8ffa-32b09d1431cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-161908" :question/problem "![](images/2f/2fff2eab1f5670dfcd3351dcbb9be1b3110e6ebdcd452cf4c5a273611d454c8b.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨1,\\,0,\\,x⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of the solid onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=0$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=-\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper plane ${S}_{1}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is upward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨1,0,x⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower hemisphere ${S}_{2}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=1,\\,Q=0,\\,R=x.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-1\\cdot \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-0\\cdot \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}+x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-161908/ma-source-field/26786b9518b2d04b1b51f18aca484f13e91fbc7127a40446f8f469d15c08e7a5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
