 {:db/id "q-36754/ma-source-field/8f8617a929e5970327a9626eb63d6f882d7d679857ce81fd5eb984ea51f373df" :answer-field/id #uuid "fa0a835b-df77-526f-b20f-c5410f491aab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-36754/ma-source-field/8f8617a929e5970327a9626eb63d6f882d7d679857ce81fd5eb984ea51f373df/e9d21781d3bdf1d6d9cbe761e0321b838ab6af049a14fd085e62c095f008173d" "q-36754/ma-source-field/8f8617a929e5970327a9626eb63d6f882d7d679857ce81fd5eb984ea51f373df/c04b5bb5b1767bc9bc0a0f7233f944df6ee218e436b739becb5806a8474357da" "q-36754/ma-source-field/8f8617a929e5970327a9626eb63d6f882d7d679857ce81fd5eb984ea51f373df/dbaa09240685cbab70733d16ceb7035bbb6c403a93b2ca40949eaf81062d92a8" "q-36754/ma-source-field/8f8617a929e5970327a9626eb63d6f882d7d679857ce81fd5eb984ea51f373df/dec9d6ec669c20ec828f8233129ad1cee3d2e1ac03980852df5f2089a714a9cb" "q-36754/ma-source-field/8f8617a929e5970327a9626eb63d6f882d7d679857ce81fd5eb984ea51f373df/c9dbb8199039a267dc55f6f7988962ec7f673c76d8a6d36078f852119f0595bb"] :answer-field/correct "q-36754/ma-source-field/8f8617a929e5970327a9626eb63d6f882d7d679857ce81fd5eb984ea51f373df/c9dbb8199039a267dc55f6f7988962ec7f673c76d8a6d36078f852119f0595bb" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-36754" :question/id #uuid "a7c74ce4-3c4e-5ea4-ab88-89558065f279" :question/math-academy-id "q-36754" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the surface ${x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}=1$ at the point $(0,0,2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that we can write the equation of our surface as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}-1=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we set $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}-1$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(0,0,2)$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,0,2).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(0,0,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}-1)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}-1)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}-1)k \\\\  & =2x\\text{ }i+\\frac{2y}{9}\\text{ }j+\\frac{z}{2}\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(0,0,2) & =2(0)\\text{ }i+\\frac{2(0)}{9}\\text{ }j+\\frac{(2)}{2}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =k \\\\  & =⟨0,0,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, the vector form of the equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(0,0,2)$ and passes through the point $(0,0,2)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,2⟩+t\\,∇f(0,0,2)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,2⟩+t\\,⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-36754/ma-source-field/8f8617a929e5970327a9626eb63d6f882d7d679857ce81fd5eb984ea51f373df"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
