 {:db/id "q-51252" :question/id #uuid "cc6d7af4-796f-502e-85cf-2c3e6d5e60eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-51252" :question/problem "A tank is being filled with water. The rate of change of the volume of water in the tank $V,$ in milliliters, is directly proportional to the time $t,$ measured in hours. Additionally, the tank is also leaking $200\\,\\text{ml}$ of water every hour. What is the differential equation that describes the rate of change of the volume of water in the tank, at time $t,$ where $t$ is the time (in hours) since the tank started filling?" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change in the volume of water in the tank per hour increases in proportion to $t$ and decreases by $200.$ The differential equation that describes this is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=kt-200,\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the (positive) constant of proportionality." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-51252/ma-source-field/ed1cfffe6d12dafe6436893feb741ca569c4d3c8ce32ec3719dc0adc3001a6e5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
