 {:db/id "q-79315/ma-source-field/91354f9fe4b180e11716c93b16f9506a717d190b0eac6cbb6593a094c5889ebb" :answer-field/id #uuid "46d988ad-b19d-531c-8043-d4cc3d8d9aff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-79315/ma-source-field/91354f9fe4b180e11716c93b16f9506a717d190b0eac6cbb6593a094c5889ebb/e66168866a59358e6c6361c7c54c5e5a5a001dbe968feb56362fc9ee1a3ebda4" "q-79315/ma-source-field/91354f9fe4b180e11716c93b16f9506a717d190b0eac6cbb6593a094c5889ebb/e5314c692e477ba4d90e349d0bdca8a115c156883421f1ec9bf0e4f9724d857f" "q-79315/ma-source-field/91354f9fe4b180e11716c93b16f9506a717d190b0eac6cbb6593a094c5889ebb/8d1d9b7fedceeccb52648532ca30ce60b1b07630b6b48b5c5b9fd032654591ea" "q-79315/ma-source-field/91354f9fe4b180e11716c93b16f9506a717d190b0eac6cbb6593a094c5889ebb/8bec02f8a05fa8cd203554251217199f2437cd5ace927aa3d8b3aa950e224eb4" "q-79315/ma-source-field/91354f9fe4b180e11716c93b16f9506a717d190b0eac6cbb6593a094c5889ebb/411451d6cccd2bdde63bb534899ef10cc54346a08b5f31ba29ba4fac24287a57"] :answer-field/correct "q-79315/ma-source-field/91354f9fe4b180e11716c93b16f9506a717d190b0eac6cbb6593a094c5889ebb/411451d6cccd2bdde63bb534899ef10cc54346a08b5f31ba29ba4fac24287a57" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-79315" :question/id #uuid "ba66d242-26a0-594d-82a7-1f167d592d18" :question/math-academy-id "q-79315" :question/problem "The rate of growth of a car's displacement increases at a rate that's proportional to the time $t$ since the car started moving. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the car's displacement, $D,$ at time $t?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The rate of growth of a **car's displacement** $D(t)$ **increases** at a **rate** that's **proportional** to the **time** $t$ since the car started moving.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *car's displacement* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *interval of time* since the car started moving. In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}∝t.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the car's displacement *increases* with time, our derivative should be *positive*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}=kt.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-79315/ma-source-field/91354f9fe4b180e11716c93b16f9506a717d190b0eac6cbb6593a094c5889ebb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
