 {:db/id "q-79945/ma-source-field/856468370e1c9ffd199d00da0a74caeb3212f4adf6ac7c699925764edb6b1737" :answer-field/id #uuid "f5283d39-dcd8-5df6-aae7-c396d0691e3c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-79945/ma-source-field/856468370e1c9ffd199d00da0a74caeb3212f4adf6ac7c699925764edb6b1737/c95faf9049c3daea00037799be3b372dc3bd50e430cf0afd359cb45d4cc7edc4" "q-79945/ma-source-field/856468370e1c9ffd199d00da0a74caeb3212f4adf6ac7c699925764edb6b1737/0f38c724da2cda0724260248a76d409e8e389fadded3881809650089439ca4c4" "q-79945/ma-source-field/856468370e1c9ffd199d00da0a74caeb3212f4adf6ac7c699925764edb6b1737/4a62e499188b18189d5c98fb6c84c750a8cea578d608efcd563eae7593aebfd5" "q-79945/ma-source-field/856468370e1c9ffd199d00da0a74caeb3212f4adf6ac7c699925764edb6b1737/a770f4622be27ceb1bfd560aac163023faca76498a5b722920557944aa9bdbf9" "q-79945/ma-source-field/856468370e1c9ffd199d00da0a74caeb3212f4adf6ac7c699925764edb6b1737/d1f1d8965eecfef09ef15c57541ce976886f0d3cb09c4b1fa89403439e1bb3c7"] :answer-field/correct "q-79945/ma-source-field/856468370e1c9ffd199d00da0a74caeb3212f4adf6ac7c699925764edb6b1737/d1f1d8965eecfef09ef15c57541ce976886f0d3cb09c4b1fa89403439e1bb3c7" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-79945" :question/id #uuid "f2480529-9e51-5e8c-9f4c-f59546697173" :question/math-academy-id "q-79945" :question/problem "Use the Taylor series method to find a series solution about $x=\\pi $, in ascending powers of $(x-\\pi )$ up to and including the term in $(x-\\pi )^{3},$  of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}={y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,y(\\pi )=1,\\,{y}^{′}(\\pi )=-1.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We wish to express $y(x)$ as a Taylor series about $x=\\pi $:\n\n$$\ny(x)=y(\\pi )+(x-\\pi ){y}^{′}(\\pi )+\\frac{(x-\\pi )^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(\\pi )+\\frac{(x-\\pi )^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(\\pi )+⋯\n$$\n\nWe're given $y(\\pi )$ and ${y}^{′}(\\pi ),$ so we need to calculate ${y}^{″}(\\pi )$ and ${y}^{(3)}(\\pi ).$\n\nWe substitute $y(\\pi )=1$ into the given equation to find ${y}^{″}(\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(\\pi ) & ={y}^{2}(\\pi )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & ={1}^{2}\\cdot (0) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to find an expression for ${y}^{(3)}(x),$ we differentiate both sides on the given equation with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n{y}^{(3)}(x)=2y{y}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nWe can now find  ${y}^{(3)}(\\pi )$ by substituting in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{(3)}(\\pi ) & =2y(\\pi ){y}^{′}(\\pi )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )+{y}^{2}(\\pi )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =2(1)(-1)(0)+(1)^{2}(-1) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we  substitute  the obtained values in  the Taylor series:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =y(\\pi )+(x-\\pi ){y}^{′}(\\pi )+\\frac{(x-\\pi )^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(\\pi )+\\frac{(x-\\pi )^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(\\pi )+⋯ \\\\  & =1+(x-\\pi )\\cdot (-1)+\\frac{(x-\\pi )^{2}}{2}\\cdot (0)+\\frac{(x-\\pi )^{3}}{6}\\cdot (-1)+… \\\\  & =1-(x-\\pi )-\\frac{1}{6}(x-\\pi )^{3}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-79945/ma-source-field/856468370e1c9ffd199d00da0a74caeb3212f4adf6ac7c699925764edb6b1737"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
