 {:db/id "q-97990/ma-source-field/9551b7ff2cd1fe5ac754634b92f4e426b9cff5168f9deee5731f268d028366bb" :answer-field/id #uuid "d0d2f6ab-2224-5973-9cb7-233b0a4ecb90" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-97990/ma-source-field/9551b7ff2cd1fe5ac754634b92f4e426b9cff5168f9deee5731f268d028366bb/ca1a76cc195e72416a6a17c3721a82bd57e3e6fcc252d58bbb64913f2261c0fe" "q-97990/ma-source-field/9551b7ff2cd1fe5ac754634b92f4e426b9cff5168f9deee5731f268d028366bb/e8ae4dc235b5d2d0d8ebec0cfa30042de8990395f5022314446a4061e6a1b13e" "q-97990/ma-source-field/9551b7ff2cd1fe5ac754634b92f4e426b9cff5168f9deee5731f268d028366bb/aac5c6cedde568a86c238aa6a9e1a5aa8f28520fc3995d957496ff6c29fcb1d7" "q-97990/ma-source-field/9551b7ff2cd1fe5ac754634b92f4e426b9cff5168f9deee5731f268d028366bb/3aef26a7d7216a4abc0582bc748d56d2e1ac2768969e5d0bbe2a73a7d9dab378" "q-97990/ma-source-field/9551b7ff2cd1fe5ac754634b92f4e426b9cff5168f9deee5731f268d028366bb/9946a6591a484bfd820474c755ab3073410cd966f4d790c250ee7fd8bec9ac67"] :answer-field/correct "q-97990/ma-source-field/9551b7ff2cd1fe5ac754634b92f4e426b9cff5168f9deee5731f268d028366bb/9946a6591a484bfd820474c755ab3073410cd966f4d790c250ee7fd8bec9ac67" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-97990" :question/id #uuid "5fb95d9e-0ced-5515-baa2-9595c0717556" :question/math-academy-id "q-97990" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ and the quadratic curve ${x}^{T}\\,Ax=7.$ Given that $A$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ find the principal axes of the curve." :question/worked-solution "Notice that $A$ is symmetric. \nThe principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n{x}^{T}\\,Ax=7\n$$\n\nare the vectors that give an orthonormal eigenvector basis for $A.$ Let's now find these eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-3)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]+3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-3)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\,$ $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-97990/ma-source-field/9551b7ff2cd1fe5ac754634b92f4e426b9cff5168f9deee5731f268d028366bb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
