 {:db/id "q-97995/ma-source-field/5bf5467a8f40b4d787bc3fe1f343c230b21b41c15c5728d6c5a34303fbef1fd1" :answer-field/id #uuid "efb02de0-491f-59d6-8a75-57bfea7150b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-97995/ma-source-field/5bf5467a8f40b4d787bc3fe1f343c230b21b41c15c5728d6c5a34303fbef1fd1/c4ca8b4a1cfe1c09eb718471b7b6872c13c81218297d86cd18d0ea5e3e46f700" "q-97995/ma-source-field/5bf5467a8f40b4d787bc3fe1f343c230b21b41c15c5728d6c5a34303fbef1fd1/0d886085183df1a260e428d1bf8f690275217f82e75e86d5e87f88eebd8a758f" "q-97995/ma-source-field/5bf5467a8f40b4d787bc3fe1f343c230b21b41c15c5728d6c5a34303fbef1fd1/c00cf52e39054b9b2a40b5aac29e88cc15953defb404ef0a6003237fd552be9b" "q-97995/ma-source-field/5bf5467a8f40b4d787bc3fe1f343c230b21b41c15c5728d6c5a34303fbef1fd1/2c8dd2717b4b539cf3fd995c4237442e1fcd1094d45c8172682cf61c8e4e46de" "q-97995/ma-source-field/5bf5467a8f40b4d787bc3fe1f343c230b21b41c15c5728d6c5a34303fbef1fd1/b63bf283a78bf3276dfbd3678bb21affdeea1cd25e362531a83ef978fcac20f0"] :answer-field/correct "q-97995/ma-source-field/5bf5467a8f40b4d787bc3fe1f343c230b21b41c15c5728d6c5a34303fbef1fd1/2c8dd2717b4b539cf3fd995c4237442e1fcd1094d45c8172682cf61c8e4e46de" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-97995" :question/id #uuid "091c06c4-0cfd-5952-9df6-eba9b260f93a" :question/math-academy-id "q-97995" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ and the quadratic curve ${x}^{T}\\,Ax=7.$ Given that $A$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4,$ find the principal axes of the curve." :question/worked-solution "Notice that $A$ is symmetric. \nThe principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n{x}^{T}\\,Ax=7\n$$\n\nare the vectors that give an orthonormal eigenvector basis for $A.$ Let's now find these eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]-0[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-4)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]+4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\,$ $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-97995/ma-source-field/5bf5467a8f40b4d787bc3fe1f343c230b21b41c15c5728d6c5a34303fbef1fd1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
